= Validation <sec:validation>

In this chapter I document how I evaluated the Vakansio pipeline and what the evaluation found. The chapter has two equally important aims. The first is to describe a solid evaluation approach — split into independent layers, each measured with the right technique for its data type — that another engineer could rerun and extend. The second is to report the findings honestly. The primary result on ranking quality is inconclusive at the conventional significance level on the gold set available within the project's scope. The decomposition follows the Holistic Evaluation of Language Models framework #cite(<liangHolisticEvaluation2023>) and uses an Anthropic Claude judge where no reference answer exists to compare against #cite(<zhengJudgingLLMasaJudge2023>).

== Evaluation philosophy and methodology pivot <sec:validation:pivot>

In this section I explain how my evaluation strategy reached its current shape. My first design used one end-to-end metric — score MAE — as the main evidence for the claim that the Composite Judge improves match quality over a linear weighted sum of the sub-scores. Two independent audits found three structural problems with that metric, so I revised the design before collecting the final results. The revised design keeps the score-error metric as a calibration diagnostic and uses top-k ranking quality against independent-vendor LLM ratings as the new primary endpoint.

*Why I demoted the original score-error metric.* The score-error harness compares the pipeline's output to a deterministic ideal computed from gold inputs. The audits found three problems with this comparison:

- *Cap asymmetry.* The pipeline applies safety caps and an anti-flag penalty (@sec:design:scoring); the ideal does not. Any pipeline that ships caps is mechanically penalised on this metric.
- *Selection circularity.* The gold pair set was split on three signals — title overlap, seniority match, and skill overlap — that are the very inputs the Linear pipeline uses. The test set is engineered to favour the Linear baseline.
- *Composite Judge over-penalisation.* The Judge prompt has rule-precedence clauses that allow it to override its bounded-adjustment band on under-qualification, language gap, or family mismatch, and the post-judge clamp does not enforce the bound. This is a real calibration issue, but it is more useful as a per-axis diagnostic than as the headline result.

*The revised primary endpoint.* I adopted top-ten NDCG against independent-vendor relevance ratings as the new primary evidence. The rater is from Anthropic's Claude family; the system under evaluation runs on Google's Gemini. Using a different vendor avoids the circular reasoning the original harness had. A full multi-vendor ensemble — adding judges from a second non-Anthropic family — would strengthen the protocol further and is recorded as future work in @sec:validation:threats. My protocol follows Zheng et al. #cite(<zhengJudgingLLMasaJudge2023>): anchor-only ordinal ratings, randomised candidate order, mandatory per-rating rationale, and stratified batches across verdict buckets. The same protocol underpins the rubric-based evaluation of the bilingual reason text in @sec:validation:reason.

*What this chapter reports.* I organise the remainder of the chapter around the five evaluation layers that emerged from this design. @sec:validation:architecture describes the layered decomposition. @sec:validation:norm summarises the two structured-output normalisation layers that I froze before the methodology pivot. @sec:validation:score reports the score-error metric as a calibration diagnostic, including the per-axis bias decomposition. @sec:validation:reason reports the reason-text-quality layer, including the complete prompt iteration cycle the eval pipeline guided through four versions. @sec:validation:ranking reports the primary endpoint on top-ten ranking quality, including the per-curriculum-vitae variance behind the headline confidence interval. @sec:validation:threats names the limitations of the evaluation and the specific extensions that would resolve each. A worked example tracing a single (curriculum vitae, vacancy) pair through every layer is in @sec:appendix.

== Layered evaluation architecture <sec:validation:architecture>

The pipeline produces four things per (CV, vacancy) pair: a structured CV summary, a structured vacancy analysis, a numeric score, and a bilingual reason text. My main choice: evaluate each one with a method that fits its data type, instead of squashing everything into one end-to-end number. A single metric blurs failures across stages and makes fixes harder. My decomposition follows the Holistic Evaluation of Language Models framework #cite(<liangHolisticEvaluation2023>). The five layers I use are the road map for the rest of the chapter:

- *Layer 1 — CV normalisation.* Structured JSON, graded against my own reference normalisations. Headline: per-field F1 = 0.896 on 25 CVs. Frozen after nine prompt versions.
- *Layer 2 — Vacancy normalisation.* Structured JSON, graded against my own reference normalisations. Headline: per-field overall = 0.810 on 355 of 357 vacancies. Frozen after twelve prompt versions.
- *Layer 3 — Score.* Numeric scalar in $[0, 1]$ compared to a computed ideal from gold inputs. Headline: Linear MAE = 0.101, Composite MAE = 0.187 on 392 pairs. Kept as a calibration diagnostic (see @sec:validation:score).
- *Layer 4 — Reason text.* Bilingual natural language, judged by Claude Opus 4.7 on a six-dimension rubric. Headline: overall rubric mean = 8.35 / 10 on 391 pairs. Prompt v6 wins $+0.30$ over the v4 baseline.
- *Layer 5 — Ranking.* Top-ten ordered list, judged by Claude Sonnet 4.6 on a single match-quality score. Headline: $Delta$ NDCG\@10 = $-0.047$, 95% CI $[-0.168, +0.065]$ on 14 CVs. Inconclusive at $alpha = 0.05$.

Each layer owns its own failures. A misclassified vacancy is a Layer 2 problem regardless of the score. A weak reason on a correctly scored pair is a Layer 4 problem. A stable score but a worse top-ten ordering is a Layer 5 problem. Each layer also uses the method the literature treats as standard for that data type: hand-annotated references with field-level scoring for the two structured layers, a computed ideal for the numeric layer, LLM-as-Judge on a rubric for the natural-language layer #cite(<zhengJudgingLLMasaJudge2023>), and ordering-quality metrics (NDCG, Spearman, Kendall) against per-pair relevance ratings for the ranking layer #cite(<jarvelinCumulatedGainBased2002>). The same separation Clean Architecture imposes on the production code (@sec:design:clean-arch) organises the evaluation: the Domain types the scoring service consumes are exactly the inputs my eval tool feeds to each layer. The eval tool, a separate .NET project under #raw("EvalTool/"), runs offline against snapshots of the gold data and shares the production interfaces, so it tests the same code paths that ship to users.

== Layers 1 and 2 — frozen normalisation baselines <sec:validation:norm>

In this section I report the two structured-output normalisation layers, both of which I closed before the methodology pivot in @sec:validation:pivot and against whose frozen state the later layers compose. Both layers follow the same shape: a gold set of author-curated reference normalisations, a battery of per-field graders applied to the pipeline output, and an append-only history journal that records every iteration of the prompt or post-processor.

*Layer 1 — curriculum-vitae normalisation.* The gold set comprises twenty-five curricula vitae, which I normalised into the same JSON shape that the production pipeline persists as #raw("UserProfile.CvSummary"). Nineteen field-level scored metrics are computed by the graders declared in #raw("EvalTool/Grading/EvaluationEngine.cs"): F1 for skill list comparisons (#raw("technical_skills"), #raw("domain_skills"), #raw("unverified_skills")), Jaccard overlap for target-role lists, exact-match for categorical fields (seniority, work format), and an ordinal-distance grader on the Common European Framework of Reference ladder (A1–C2) for English level. The headline number is the macro-averaged score across these metrics. The current production CV-normalisation service reaches an overall score of 0.896 on this gold set after nine prompt versions; I preserve the iteration history in #raw("eval_history/runs.csv"). I consider the layer frozen because subsequent runs change the headline number by less than the per-iteration noise observed across the last three iterations.

*Layer 2 — vacancy normalisation.* The gold set comprises three hundred and fifty-seven vacancies I sampled across the seven source adapters and stratified to cover four heuristic strata (Strong, Partial, Weak, Mismatch) that align with the Composite Judge verdict bucket names. I normalised each vacancy into the same JSON shape that the production pipeline persists as #raw("JobVacancy.VacancyAnalysisJson"), and seventeen field-level scored metrics are computed by the graders declared in #raw("EvalTool/Grading/VacancyEvaluationEngine.cs"), adapted to the vacancy schema (must-have skill lists, nice-to-have skill lists, domain, seniority, work format, language requirement). The current production vacancy-normalisation service reaches an overall field-weighted score of 0.810 across three hundred and fifty-five of the three hundred and fifty-seven gold vacancies (two were excluded by the loader because of malformed source records) after twelve prompt versions; the iteration history is preserved in #raw("eval_history/vacancy/runs.csv"). I consider the layer frozen on the same noise-threshold criterion as Layer 1.

*What the frozen baselines fix, and what they do not measure.* Because Layers 1 and 2 are frozen, all the later layers run against a known, reproducible upstream. I report Layer 3 and Layer 5 results both against the production normalisation services and (in parallel) against the gold normalisations directly, which gives an upper bound on what the pipeline could reach with perfect upstream. Layer 4 is reported only against the production path because reason text is generated after scoring. Two caveats. First, per-field F1 measures how well the pipeline matches my own gold annotation — not how well the annotation matches reality. Both gold sets were curated by a single annotator (me), and I did not collect an inter-annotator coefficient. Second, the fields with lowest F1 — the skill lists, between roughly 0.47 and 0.62 — are the same fields the scoring formula leans on hardest. I come back to both points in @sec:validation:threats.

== Layer 3 — score as a calibration diagnostic <sec:validation:score>

In this section I report the score-error layer and explain why I keep it as a calibration diagnostic rather than as the primary evidence for the thesis claim. The layer measures how closely the pipeline's end-to-end composite score tracks a deterministic ideal computed on gold inputs. The harness was my original primary endpoint; the methodology pivot in @sec:validation:pivot moved it to a diagnostic role after two independent audits found three structural issues that make the metric a poor fit for the question the thesis actually asks.

*The harness.* For every (curriculum vitae, vacancy) pair in a fixed three-hundred-and-ninety-two-pair test set, the harness loads the gold CV summary and the gold vacancy analysis, runs the seven sub-score calculators on those gold inputs, and clamps the weighted sum to $[0, 1]$. The resulting #raw("ideal_score") is the raw weighted sum with no anti-flag penalty, no missing-must-have soft penalty, no language-gap cap, and no family-mismatch cap. The harness then reads the pipeline's end-to-end output for the same pair and reports the absolute difference. The harness is implemented in #raw("EvalTool/Grading/ScoringEvaluationEngine.cs") and is run through the orchestrator entry point #raw("ScoringEvalOrchestrator.cs").

*First-look numbers.* On the 392-pair test set, the Linear pipeline (weighted sum of the seven sub-scores, no Composite Judge) reaches 0.899 overall — MAE 0.101, verdict-bucket match 67.3 per cent. The Composite pipeline (Judge invoked on every pair) reaches 0.813 — MAE 0.187, verdict-bucket match 31.9 per cent. The naive reading says the Composite Judge is worse than the Linear baseline. The two audits show why that reading is misleading.

*Three audit findings (in summary).* I summarise the three structural issues here; the full audit decomposition is in #raw("Capstone Project/EVAL_VALIDITY_APPENDIX.md").

- *Cap asymmetry.* The pipeline applies the safety caps and anti-flag penalty from @sec:design:scoring; the ideal does not. A per-pair decomposition reveals that approximately fourteen per cent of pairs carry an active anti-flag and drift by an average of $-0.379$ against the ideal, while the remaining eighty-six per cent without an anti-flag drift by an average of $-0.056$. Together these account for almost all of the Linear pipeline's headline error: $0.14 times 0.379 + 0.86 times 0.056 = 0.101$, matching the Linear MAE exactly. So the metric measures the caps, not pipeline quality.
- *Selection circularity.* I stratified the 392-pair test set on title overlap, seniority match, and skill overlap — the same primitives the Linear pipeline's #raw("RoleIntentMatchCalculator") and #raw("SkillMatchCalculator") consume. The Linear pipeline is graded on its home turf. The audit estimates this asymmetry inflates the Linear advantage by 0.02–0.04 MAE points (40–50 per cent of the visible gap).
- *Composite Judge over-penalisation.* The Judge prompt's rule zero requires output within $plus.minus 0.05$ of the input linear score, but rules three (under-qualification), four (language cap), and five (different-family cap) explicitly override rule zero when triggered, and the post-Judge clamp in #raw("GeminiCompositeJudgeService.cs") enforces $[0, 1]$ but not the $plus.minus 0.05$ band. The five largest Linear-to-Composite divergences are $-0.39, -0.38, -0.26, -0.23, -0.21$, all on pairs where one of the three override rules fired. The fix is straightforward: enforce a hard $abs(s_"judge" - s_"linear") <= 0.10$ clamp at the boundary.

*What remains after the artefacts are subtracted.* Of the visible 0.086 gap between the Linear and Composite headlines, the audits attribute roughly 0.04 to eval-engine artefacts (cap asymmetry plus selection circularity) and roughly 0.05 to the Composite over-penalisation. Subtract both and the Composite Judge would still underperform on the score-tracking metric, but the gap would be about half what the headline suggests. I confirm this empirically in @sec:validation:ranking: running the saved Composite scores through a post-hoc $plus.minus 0.10$ clamp collapses the largest negative outlier from about $-0.54$ to essentially zero.

*Why this metric answers the wrong question.* The deeper point: score-tracking is the wrong primary endpoint. The thesis claim is about ranking quality — whether the Composite top-ten is better-ordered against human-judged relevance than the Linear top-ten. A pipeline can have a higher MAE against the linear ideal while still ordering its top ten better. I replaced the primary endpoint; the new endpoint is in @sec:validation:ranking. The score-tracking harness stays because the per-sub-score bias breakdown it produces — for example, $+0.028$ on #raw("DomainAlignment") and $+0.045$ on #raw("RoleIntentMatch") — is useful when I rebalance a sub-score calculator.

== Layer 4 — reason-text quality and the prompt iteration cycle <sec:validation:reason>

In this section I report the reason-text-quality layer, which evaluates the bilingual English and Ukrainian explanations the pipeline generates for the top-thirty candidate vacancies in every result. The data type here is short natural-language text, so the field-grader method used in Layers 1 and 2 does not work. My methodology is large-language-model judging on an independent Anthropic Claude family on a structured rubric, following the protocol of Zheng et al. #cite(<zhengJudgingLLMasaJudge2023>) and the domain-specific rubric design of Hu et al. for hiring explanations #cite(<huAiHiringLLMs2025>).

*The judge.* My judge model is Claude Opus 4.7 from Anthropic. The choice of an Anthropic judge avoids the within-system bias that a same-family judge would carry: the model whose outputs are being rated is Google's Gemini 2.5 Flash, so the judge and the system under test come from different vendor families. Opus is the strongest model in the Anthropic family at the time of the evaluation; Zheng et al. recommend using the strongest tier on the judge side in an asymmetric setup. Using a single judge family is a deliberate scope choice, not the final word on method: I record a full multi-vendor ensemble as the principal limitation in @sec:validation:threats.

*The rubric.* Each (candidate, vacancy) reason text is rated on six dimensions. Four are ordinal on the six-point anchor set $\{0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10\}$: #raw("factual_accuracy") (do the strengths, gaps, and context claims correspond to the actual CV and vacancy content?), #raw("completeness") (are the top factual signals surfaced?), #raw("specificity") (is the phrasing pair-unique rather than generic boilerplate?), and #raw("relevance_for_user") (is the explanation actionable for an apply-or-skip decision?). Two are binary: #raw("verdict_match") (does the verdict word agree with the score bucket?) and #raw("uk_term_preservation") (are technical brand-and-tool tokens preserved in Latin script in the Ukrainian half?). I record the rubric, the anchor descriptions, and worked examples in #raw("docs/research/reason_eval_rubric.md").

*Bias mitigation in the judging protocol.* I apply five mitigations per Zheng et al. I anonymise candidate identifiers at random (candidate A versus candidate B, with a random mapping back to the actual prompt version being tested). I instruct the judge not to compare candidates against one another within a pair, only against the rubric. The ordinal dimensions use anchor-only scoring with no intermediate values. Pairs are organised into batches of twenty-eight stratified proportionally across the four verdict buckets. The judge emits a per-rating rationale alongside each numeric score, for human inspection and downstream auditability.

*Does the judge agree with itself? — pilot check.* Before running the main ratings, I called the Claude Opus 4.7 judge twice on the same ten pilot pairs, with randomised order and non-zero temperature, and computed Krippendorff's $alpha$ across the rubric. This number says how often the judge agrees with itself across two runs — it is *not* a check that two different vendors agree with each other. My threshold was $alpha gt.eq 0.6$ for ordinal dimensions and $alpha gt.eq 0.7$ for binary ones, slightly below the standard Krippendorff (2004) threshold of 0.667. I used the lower bar because this check is meant to catch a wildly unstable judge, not to formally prove rater reliability. Mean ordinal $alpha$ on the pilot was 0.695, with binary raw agreement between 93 and 97 per cent. The lowest per-dimension $alpha$ — #raw("factual_accuracy") at $alpha = 0.603$ — was 0.003 below the threshold; I report it openly. A real cross-vendor inter-rater check remains the main limitation (@sec:validation:threats).

*Author spot-check of the gold ratings.* I did not treat the judge ratings as a black box. I picked a stratified sample of pairs across the four verdict buckets and read each one end-to-end — the CV, the vacancy, the judge's score on every dimension, and the rationale. The point was a sanity check, not a re-annotation. The judge's ratings still served as the gold values in the statistical inference. On the spot-checked pairs I saw no obvious bias pattern, but I want to be clear: I am the author of the project under evaluation, so I am not a disinterested rater, and I report the spot-check for transparency, not as an independent gold standard.

*Statistical inference protocol.* For each prompt-version comparison, my headline number is the paired difference of the per-pair overall mean across all six dimensions. Confidence intervals come from paired bootstrap with ten thousand resamples. Per-dimension deltas are reported separately as exploratory signals.

*The prompt iteration cycle.* I demonstrate a complete detect-fix-validate-iterate loop through four prompt versions, each developed in response to a regression that the previous iteration surfaced. @tab:validation:prompt-iter summarises the comparison endpoints.

