The per-version story in one sentence each: *v4* added context-lead phrasing and a #raw("ReasonContext") record from #raw("ScoringServiceV2.BuildReasonContext()") with a #raw("ReasonValidator") safety net, gaining $+0.55$ overall over v2 but regressing #raw("factual_accuracy") by $-0.43$ when the language model inferred labels from its own judgement; *v5* rewrote the context-lead rule to forbid inference and require strict #raw("ReasonContext") referencing, recovering #raw("factual_accuracy") by $+0.71$ but regressing #raw("completeness") by $-0.62$ and #raw("relevance_for_user") by $-0.37$ as the model became too cautious; *v6* replaced free-form context generation with four constrained templates (Role-Family Mismatch, Cross-Domain, Overqualification, Underqualification) that interpolate literal #raw("ReasonContext") values into fixed sentence shapes, producing significant gains on all four ordinal dimensions (#raw("factual_accuracy") $+0.50$, #raw("completeness") $+0.23$, #raw("specificity") $+0.77$, #raw("relevance_for_user") $+0.31$, overall $+0.30$) and is the production version. The iteration cycle itself is a contribution: each detection was a confidence-interval calculation, not a guess, and each fix was a single component change with the rest of the pipeline held still.

== Layer 5 — top-ten ranking quality (the primary endpoint) <sec:validation:ranking>

In this section I report the primary endpoint of the thesis: top-ten Normalised Discounted Cumulative Gain (NDCG\@10) #cite(<jarvelinCumulatedGainBased2002>) of the Composite pipeline against the Linear baseline, measured on a per-(CV, candidate-set) basis with paired-bootstrap confidence intervals. The endpoint is what the original thesis claim is actually about — whether the Composite pipeline produces a better-ordered top-ten list than the Linear pipeline — and I adopted it after the methodology pivot in @sec:validation:pivot.

*The judge.* For this layer my judge is Claude Sonnet 4.6 from Anthropic, distinct from the Claude Opus 4.7 judge in @sec:validation:reason. The choice of the more cost-effective tier reflects the task: each per-pair rating is a single ordinal value on the anchor set $\{0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10\}$ rather than the six-dimension rubric Layer 4 requires, and I need the ratings at scale (391 pairs across 14 CVs). The cross-vendor configuration is preserved — an Anthropic judge rates the output of the Gemini-backed Vakansio pipeline — and the same anchor-only ordinal protocol with mandatory per-rating rationale and randomised candidate order is applied. The single-vendor judge limitation is named explicitly in @sec:validation:threats.

*The rated quantity.* The judge rates each (CV, vacancy) pair on a single dimension named #raw("match_quality"). Anchor descriptions span the range from "no signal of fit between the candidate and the vacancy" at zero to "the candidate is an exceptional match for the role as described" at ten. Pairs are presented to the judge with the full CV summary and vacancy analysis but without either pipeline's score; the rating is therefore a relevance signal independent of either system under test.

*Author spot-check of the match-quality ratings.* I subjected the match-quality gold set to the same plausibility audit described in @sec:validation:reason — a stratified sample across verdict buckets, read end-to-end against the supporting evidence — and recorded no rater-flagged bias pattern. The same absence-of-evidence limitation and the same single-rater interest in the outcome apply.

*The endpoint and the test.* For each of the 14 CVs, I independently rank the candidate vacancy set (approximately 28 vacancies per CV, stratified across verdict buckets) by the Composite pipeline and by the Linear pipeline, and score the top ten of each against the judge's match-quality ratings using the standard NDCG\@10 definition with $log_2$ discounting. The per-CV primary statistic is the difference between the Composite and Linear NDCG\@10. The aggregate statistic is the mean of the per-CV differences, with a 95 per cent paired-bootstrap confidence interval over the 14 CVs (10,000 resamples).

*The headline result.* The mean of the per-CV differences is $Delta "NDCG"@10 = -0.047$, with a 95 per cent paired-bootstrap confidence interval of $[-0.168, +0.065]$. The interval crosses zero. Cohen's $d$ on the per-CV differences is $-0.199$ — a small directional effect favouring the Linear pipeline. The per-CV outcome is five Composite wins, nine Linear wins, no ties. My honest reading: the thesis claim that the Composite Judge improves ranking quality is neither supported nor refuted on this gold set. The data warrant "directionally non-significant" — not "wins", not "loses".

*Both pipelines rank well on the full ordering.* When I look at the full ordering (not just the top ten), both pipelines correlate strongly with the judge's relevance ratings. Per-CV Spearman correlation is $rho = +0.690$ for Composite and $rho = +0.691$ for Linear; Kendall's $tau$ is $+0.481$ and $+0.503$. Both are far above what a random ranker would produce. The underlying scoring methodology works regardless of whether the Composite Judge stage is enabled, and the two pipelines are statistically indistinguishable on the full ordering too.

*The headline hides a big spread between CVs.* The Composite pipeline wins on some CVs and loses on others. It wins where the Judge's qualitative call changes the ordering for the better — a senior QA automation CV ($Delta = +0.331$), a frontend-engineer CV with React vs. Angular candidates ($Delta = +0.158$), and my own career-switching product-manager CV ($Delta = +0.199$). It loses where the deterministic skill match is already strong and the Judge adds noise — a broad full-stack CV where the Judge over-penalises depth specialisation ($Delta = -0.643$), an engineering-manager CV ($Delta = -0.263$), and a senior PM CV ($Delta = -0.172$). The single broad-full-stack CV alone drives most of the negative aggregate. Remove it and the mean delta drops to about $-0.001$ — essentially a tie. I keep the outlier in the headline because cutting inconvenient data is itself risky, but I flag it openly here so the reader can judge it.

*Sensitivity check under the proposed calibration clamp.* The fix from @sec:validation:score is a hard $plus.minus 0.10$ clamp at the boundary of #raw("GeminiCompositeJudgeService"). The clamp is a simple post-processing filter on the Judge's output. The Composite and Linear scores are already saved per pair, so I apply the clamp arithmetically to those saved scores. There is no need to re-run the Gemini Judge calls: the clamp does not change what the model decides, only the value the boundary returns. I apply it per pair as $s'_"composite" = max(s_"linear" - 0.10, min(s_"linear" + 0.10, s_"composite"))$ and recompute NDCG\@10 against the unchanged Sonnet ratings. The sensitivity analysis reproduces the published $Delta = -0.047$ within rounding as $Delta = -0.040$. Under the clamp the mean moves to $Delta = +0.018$, with a 95 per cent CI of $[-0.054, +0.091]$ that still crosses zero. The sign flips from negative to positive, but the verdict — "directionally non-significant" — does not change. The largest outlier, the broad-full-stack CV #raw("17_realformat_swe_fullstack"), drops from about $-0.54$ to $approx 0$ under the clamp, confirming the audit's attribution to the rule-precedence over-penalisation. The per-CV outcome shifts from 5–9 to 7–7. I record deploying the fix as future work in @sec:conclusion because production deployment needs a fresh pipeline run; the point reported here is that the methodology's verdict holds whether the fix ships or not.

*What this layer does and does not claim.* The layer does not claim that the Composite Judge improves ranking quality. It does not claim the opposite either. The interval crosses zero. What the layer does claim is that the methodology is sound: independent-vendor LLM judging on an anchor-only relevance scale, NDCG\@10 with paired-bootstrap intervals, per-CV variance reported openly, and the largest negative case attributed to a fixable calibration defect. The current answer is honestly inconclusive on this gold set. The conditions under which the answer would change are in @sec:validation:threats. A full end-to-end worked example tracing a single (CV, vacancy) pair through all five layers is in @sec:appendix.

== Layer 6 — held-out validation against a fresh-pair, fresh-vacancy gold set <sec:validation:heldout>

In this section I report the strongest evidence the thesis has on the recruiter-side scoring pipeline: an evaluation against a held-out gold set of 398 (curriculum vitae, vacancy) pairs that the production prompt never saw during iteration. The gold set was built in two stages described in @sec:validation:heldout:methodology — an initial pair-level held-out (131 pairs drawn from the development vacancy pool) and a fresh-vacancy extension that scraped and normalised 300 new vacancies after the production prompt was frozen, then paired them with the 25 curriculum-vitae pool. By the final stage, $126 / 398 = 31.7%$ of all pairs use vacancies the production prompt has provably never seen, in addition to the 11 curricula vitae in the safety subset that have provably never been used during prompt iteration. The held-out layer closes the *selection-circularity*, *single-annotator*, and *vacancy-pool partial leakage* threats named in @sec:validation:threats, and provides the first controlled ablation of the production #raw("ScoringCapService") against the bare language-model composite.

The headline result on the held-out set is that the production scoring system achieves Spearman $rho = 0.648$ ($95%$ CI $[0.580, 0.712]$) and Quadratic Weighted Kappa $kappa_Q = 0.638$ against the Opus gold, with mean absolute error $1.638$ on the native ordinal 0–10 scale ($95%$ CI $[1.533, 1.745]$), and Normalised Discounted Cumulative Gain $"NDCG"@5 = 0.82$ averaged across the 25 curricula vitae used as queries. On the *midrange filter* — pairs where the gold rating is non-trivial ($\{4, 6, 8, 10\}$, $n = 110$) — the Spearman correlation rises to $rho = 0.738$ and the mean absolute error falls to $1.13$.

=== Methodology <sec:validation:heldout:methodology>

*Three-subset held-out design.* A held-out gold set has a different risk profile from a development set: every pair must be one the prompt was never tuned against. The design I used has three subsets each addressing a distinct failure mode. The *safety subset* ($n = 189$) consists of eleven curricula vitae from professional families that never appeared in development (junior product designer, junior DevOps engineer, HR recruiter, junior nurse, senior cardiologist, corporate lawyer, senior English teacher, senior accountant, growth marketer, academic professor of humanities, mid security engineer), each paired with approximately seventeen vacancies sampled from across the full 657-vacancy pool, biased toward technology roles to test catastrophic-mismatch detection. The *coverage and strong-fit subset* ($n = 209$) consists of the 14 curricula vitae used in development plus the same 11 fresh curricula vitae, paired with vacancies stratified across role families (same family, adjacent family, cross-family, same-seniority-same-family strong fit); each pair is novel and a large fraction use the fresh-vacancy extension described below. The *midrange filter* ($n = 110$) is a cross-cutting filter over the above two subsets restricted to pairs where the gold rating falls in $\{4, 6, 8, 10\}$ — the regime where the recruiter's decision between candidates actually happens.

*Two-stage dataset construction.* The held-out gold was built in two stages, each addressing a distinct subset of the threats named in @sec:validation:threats. Stage 1 produced an intra-pool held-out (initially 131, then 272 pairs after a first expansion) drawn from the existing 357-vacancy production pool; the Stage-1 gold addresses the *selection-circularity* threat — the prompt did not see these specific pairings during iteration — but it does not address *vacancy-pool partial leakage* because the vacancy text itself was seen during normalisation prompt iteration. Stage 2 added a fresh-vacancy extension of 126 pairs: 1,543 new vacancies were scraped from #raw("work.ua") and #raw("djinni.co") using non-tech queries (legal, healthcare, HR, marketing, finance, sales, design, education, security), the first 300 normalised via the same #raw("IVacancyExtractionService") the production API uses, and the 300 fresh vacancies paired with the 25 curricula vitae stratified across professional families. The production prompt provably did not see the vacancy text for these pairs during iteration. This stage retires the *vacancy-pool partial leakage* threat for these 126 pairs and shifts the overall ratio of fresh-vacancy pairs from $0%$ to $31.7%$ of the held-out gold.

*Rater protocol.* Each pair was rated independently by Claude Opus 4.7 — a different model tier and a different generation than the Sonnet 4.6 judge used in @sec:validation:ranking — on the anchor-only ordinal scale $\{0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10\}$ with mandatory per-rating rationale, following Zheng et al. #cite(<zhengJudgingLLMasaJudge2023>). The rating protocol matches the Layer 5 protocol in every respect except the model tier and the dataset.

*Test-retest reliability.* A 30-pair stratified subsample of the held-out pairs was independently re-rated by the same Opus checkpoint with a rephrased rubric — the anchor descriptions were rewritten in alternative phrasings without changing the underlying ordinal semantics. The two passes yield Spearman $rho = 0.988$, exact-match agreement $89.7%$, within-$plus.minus 1$-anchor agreement $100%$, and mean absolute error $0.21$ on the native 0–10 scale. The test-retest correlation is publication-grade and bounds the upper limit of any correlation a system-under-test can achieve against this gold set: no scoring system can match a single-rater gold above the rater's own self-consistency.

*Production-system comparison.* For each pair I ran the production recruiter-side scoring service #raw("RecruiterMonolithicScoringService") at prompt version #raw("scoring_monolithic_recruiter_v1_6_source_weighting"). The full set of 398 predictions took $200$ seconds at concurrency four and cost approximately $\$1.00$ in Gemini 2.5 Flash invocations (2.4M input tokens, 109K output tokens). I record #raw("Score"), #raw("SubScores"), #raw("AntiFlagPenalty"), #raw("Confidence"), and the bilingual #raw("Reason") for each pair; the #raw("Score") field stores the raw linear composite (weighted sum of seven sub-scores multiplied by the anti-flag penalty), before #raw("ScoringCapService") applies any structural caps.

*Baselines.* Two classical-information-retrieval baselines were computed on the same 398 pairs without invoking any large language model: TF-IDF cosine over character-level word-boundary n-grams (range 3–5) of the concatenated curriculum-vitae and vacancy structured fields, with sub-linear term-frequency weighting and smoothed inverse-document frequency; and BM25 Okapi (k1=1.5, b=0.75) with per-curriculum-vitae min-max normalisation. Both baselines are implemented in pure .NET 8 inside the EvalTool project and require no external Python dependency.

*Cross-vendor configuration.* The system under test (Gemini 2.5 Flash, Google) and the rater (Claude Opus 4.7, Anthropic) come from different vendor families. The configuration mitigates — though does not eliminate — the same-vendor self-consensus risk named as a threat in @sec:validation:threats. The single-rater family limitation nevertheless remains and is named again in @sec:validation:heldout:limits.

