) <tab:heldout:reliability>

*The raw composite is systematically over-confident across the mid- and upper distribution.* Nine of the ten populated bins show the predicted mean exceeding the gold mean; only the lowest bin ($[0.0, 0.1)$, $n = 3$) is essentially calibrated (and slightly under-reports by $0.9$ percentage points). A pair the system reports as $25%$ in fact lands at $9%$; a pair reported as $53%$ in fact lands at $40%$; a pair reported as $74%$ lands at $56%$. The recruiter who reads the displayed percentages is reading inflated values by between nine and nineteen percentage points across the over-confident bins.

*Production-grade mitigation via post-hoc isotonic regression.* The over-confidence is corrected in production by a post-hoc isotonic-regression calibrator fitted offline on the held-out gold via the #raw("EvalTool fit-calibration") command. The fitter applies Pool-Adjacent-Violators isotonic regression and (independently) Platt scaling, evaluates both via 5-fold cross-validation on the same held-out, and persists the better of the two as a portable JSON artefact with the fitted knots. The chosen calibrator achieves $5$-fold cross-validated Expected Calibration Error of $0.027$ on this gold — an $80%$ relative reduction from the raw $0.138$. The artefact is loaded once at production startup by #raw("CalibratorLoader") and applied to every raw composite via #raw("IScoreCalibrator.Calibrate") before the recruiter UI displays the percentage. Runtime cost is one binary search over knots plus linear interpolation — sub-microsecond per call. *Calibration as a thesis-named limitation is therefore mitigated in production, not merely documented as future work.*

=== Caps on/off ablation <sec:validation:heldout:caps-ablation>

The production scoring pipeline applies #raw("ScoringCapService") after the language model emits the seven sub-scores; the service applies rule-based caps for seniority gap, experience gap, language gap, a combined experience+seniority cap, and a domain-alignment subtractor. The caps are an explicit architectural decision documented in @sec:design:scoring. The held-out evaluation provides the first controlled ablation of this decision: apply the caps offline to the recorded raw composite scores using each pair's recorded sub-scores and re-derived language-gap signal, then re-compute every metric.

#figure(
  table(
    columns: 4,
    align: (left, right, right, right),
    table.header[Metric][Caps OFF][Caps ON][$Delta$],
    [Spearman $rho$], [$0.648$], [$0.592$], [$-0.056$ ($-8.6%$)],
    [Kendall $tau$], [$0.524$], [$0.495$], [$-0.029$],
    [Quadratic Weighted Kappa], [$0.638$], [$0.632$], [$-0.006$ (essentially tied)],
    [Mean absolute error (0–10)], [$1.638$], [*$1.584$*], [$-0.054$ ($-3.3%$)],
    [$"NDCG"@3$], [$0.813$], [$0.806$], [$-0.007$ (preserved)],
    [$"NDCG"@5$], [$0.822$], [$0.799$], [$-0.023$ ($-2.8%$)],
    [Expected Calibration Error], [$0.138$], [*$0.118$*], [$-0.020$ ($-14.5%$)],
  ),
  caption: [Side-by-side metrics for the held-out evaluation with #raw("ScoringCapService") disabled (Caps OFF, raw language-model composite) and enabled (Caps ON, production behaviour). Caps fired on $304 / 398$ pairs ($76.4%$). Bold = better cell per metric. The caps substantially improve calibration ($-14.5%$ Expected Calibration Error, $-3.3%$ Mean Absolute Error) at essentially zero cost to top-of-list ranking ($"NDCG"@3$ moves by $0.007$, ordinal-rater agreement (QWK) moves by $0.006$). The pairwise rank correlation cost ($-0.056$ Spearman) is the trade-off the design accepts.]
) <tab:heldout:caps-ablation>

*The trade-off is interpretable.* Caps map distinct raw composite values onto identical capped values whenever the cap fires — a pair with raw composite $0.55$ and another with raw composite $0.62$ both land at $0.25$ if both trigger the combined experience+seniority cap. The mapping destroys pairwise rank information between similarly-bad pairs and Spearman drops accordingly. In return, the capped value is closer to the gold rating on average: pairs that scored $0.55$ raw when they should have been $0.20$ now show $0.25$, much closer to the gold. Mean absolute error drops by $3.3%$; Expected Calibration Error drops by $14.5%$; ordinal agreement (QWK) is preserved within noise; and top-of-list ranking (NDCG\@3) moves by $0.007$ — within the noise floor of the metric.

*The reading.* The #raw("ScoringCapService") is empirically a net positive on the metrics most relevant to the production use case. Calibration error — the most consequential failure mode when a recruiter reads a number on screen and treats it at face value — drops by $14.5%$. Top-of-list ranking is preserved. The cost is an $8.6%$ reduction in pairwise rank correlation, which the recruiter workflow does not directly expose. The architectural decision to apply caps is empirically justified on the held-out evidence; combined with the post-hoc isotonic calibrator in @sec:validation:heldout:calibration, total Expected Calibration Error in production is reduced from the raw $0.138$ to under $0.03$.

=== Limitations of the held-out evaluation <sec:validation:heldout:limits>

The held-out evaluation resolves several threats named in @sec:validation:threats but introduces or fails to retire others.

*Single-rater family.* All 398 ratings were produced by Claude Opus 4.7. The configuration is cross-vendor (the rater is Anthropic, the system under test is Google) and is therefore stronger than the intra-vendor configuration used in Layer 5, but it remains intra-family on the rater side. A multi-rater ensemble — adding an OpenAI GPT model and a Mistral or DeepSeek model as additional independent raters and reporting inter-rater Krippendorff's $alpha$ #cite(<krippendorffComputingKrippendorffsAlphareliability2011>) — would yield a published-grade inter-vendor methodology. The extension is recorded as future work in @sec:conclusion.

*Distribution skew of the safety subset.* Approximately half of the held-out set ($189 / 398 = 47.5%$) is in the safety subset where the gold rating is heavily concentrated at $0$ or $2$. The skew makes the aggregate metrics partly a measurement of how well the systems detect catastrophic mismatches, an arguably easier task than the recruiter's actual workload. The subset breakdown in @tab:heldout:subsets explicitly separates this regime.

*Prompt version ablation.* The pre-#raw("v1_6") prompt history was not preserved in version control; consequently the held-out evaluation reports the production prompt only and does not include a controlled #raw("v1_5") vs #raw("v1_6") comparison. The version-control gap is recorded as a process limitation in @sec:conclusion.

== Threats to validity <sec:validation:threats>

In this section I name the limitations of the evaluation and the specific extensions that would resolve each of them, in order from the most consequential to the least. Three threats named in earlier drafts of this chapter have been substantially mitigated by Layer 6 and are recorded here as retired or partially retired.

*Selection circularity (retired by Layer 6).* The Layer 3 score-error metric and the Layer 5 ranking gold both used the 391-pair set assembled alongside prompt iteration, leaving open the question of whether the reported numbers reflect prompt quality or convenient evaluation selection. The held-out evaluation in @sec:validation:heldout — 398 pairs the production prompt never saw, against ratings produced by a different Anthropic checkpoint than the Layer 5 judge — provides evidence on this question. The aggregate finding (Spearman $rho = 0.65$ against the Opus gold) and the subset breakdown (@tab:heldout:subsets) are both reported on a controlled-distribution held-out and do not share the selection-circularity threat.

*Vacancy-pool partial leakage (retired by Layer 6 Stage 2).* The Stage-1 held-out used pairs that were novel but drew vacancy text from the development pool, so the model had seen each vacancy during normalisation prompt iteration. The Stage-2 fresh-vacancy extension (@sec:validation:heldout:methodology) scraped and normalised 300 new vacancies after the production prompt was frozen and paired them with the 25-curriculum-vitae pool to produce 126 fresh-vacancy pairs. The production prompt provably did not see the vacancy text for these pairs during iteration. The threat is retired for $126 / 398 = 31.7%$ of the held-out gold.

*Calibration of the displayed percentage (mitigated in production).* The held-out reliability diagram (@tab:heldout:reliability) documents the production system's systematic over-confidence, Expected Calibration Error $0.138$. The mitigation in @sec:validation:heldout:calibration ships a post-hoc isotonic-regression calibrator that reduces 5-fold cross-validated Expected Calibration Error to $0.027$ — an $80%$ relative reduction — and is loaded at production startup by #raw("CalibratorLoader"). The recruiter UI now displays calibrated percentages whose magnitudes match the gold distribution. The threat moves from "identified, unmitigated" to "mitigated in production".

*Single-annotator gold sets (partially retired by Layer 6).* Layers 1 and 2 remain single-annotator. The held-out gold in @sec:validation:heldout uses a single LLM rater (Claude Opus 4.7) but reports test-retest reliability explicitly (Spearman $rho = 0.988$, exact-match $89.7%$, within-$plus.minus 1$-anchor $100%$). The publication-grade test-retest correlation bounds the upper limit of any system's correlation against this gold. A multi-rater inter-vendor extension remains future work and is recorded in @sec:conclusion.

*CV sample size.* The primary endpoint is computed over 14 CVs. The interval $[-0.168, +0.065]$ is wide enough that "directionally non-significant" — neither a clean win nor a clean loss — is the strongest thing the data support. A bigger gold set would either tighten the interval to exclude zero on one side or confirm the per-CV spread. I do not make per-family claims (Engineering, Data, Product, Design) at the inferential level, because no family has the 30+ CVs that a paired test would need; per-family numbers are descriptive only.

*Outlier influence on the headline delta.* The single broad-full-stack CV (#raw("17_realformat_swe_fullstack")) contributes $-0.643$ and drives most of the negative aggregate. Remove it and the mean drops to about $-0.001$. I keep the outlier in the headline because removing inconvenient data is itself a hazard, but I flag it openly here so the reader can decide how to weigh it.

*Composite Judge calibration defect.* The over-penalisation pattern documented in @sec:validation:score is the largest identifiable mechanism behind the observed negative cases on Layer 5. A hard $abs(s_"judge" - s_"linear") lt.eq 0.10$ clamp at the boundary of #raw("GeminiCompositeJudgeService") would isolate the Composite Judge's qualitative contribution from this artefact. The fix is documented and the post-hoc sensitivity check in @sec:validation:ranking quantifies its effect: applying the clamp arithmetically moves the mean delta from $Delta = -0.040$ to $Delta = +0.018$ and collapses the largest negative outlier from approximately $-0.54$ to essentially zero. Both pre- and post-clamp confidence intervals still cross zero, so the methodology's verdict of inconclusive at $alpha = 0.05$ is robust to whether the fix is deployed. Production deployment is recorded as future work in @sec:conclusion.

*Single-vendor judge family.* All judge ratings on Layers 4 and 5 originate within the Anthropic Claude family — Claude Opus 4.7 on Layer 4 and Claude Sonnet 4.6 on Layer 5. The intra-vendor configuration was a deliberate choice: it allowed a single rating pipeline and prompt format to be reused across the two layers, and the two model tiers match each layer's task. The inter-vendor extension — adding judges from at least one other vendor family, for example an OpenAI GPT model and a Mistral model — would strengthen the external validity of the Zheng et al. protocol and would let me measure inter-judge agreement directly. A Gemini Pro judge is not a suitable addition because Gemini Pro and the production Gemini 2.5 Flash share the same vendor family. I record the extension as future work in @sec:conclusion.

*Single-annotator gold sets in Layers 1, 2, and the spot-checks.* I curated the CV and vacancy gold normalisations as a single annotator. My author spot-checks of the Layer 4 and Layer 5 ratings are plausibility audits under a non-blind protocol rather than independent annotations, and I am not a disinterested rater. Inter-annotator agreement coefficients on a subset of the gold normalisations and on a subset of the match-quality ratings — by an annotator with no interest in the outcome — would be the appropriate extension; both are recorded as future work in @sec:conclusion.

*Judge-in-the-loop prompt iteration.* I used the Claude Opus 4.7 judge both while developing the Layer 4 prompts and for the final v6-versus-v4 comparison. The risk: v6 might just be better at pleasing one Anthropic checkpoint, not better at helping a human user. My defence is that the v6 prompt's gains come from hard structural changes — literal field interpolation, fixed sentence shapes, priority ordering — rather than from prose style. The per-dimension wins back this up: factual accuracy improved because the prompt forbids inference, specificity improved because of explicit #raw("vacancy_role_short") interpolation. A human comparison, or one judged by a different vendor, would confirm this and is recorded as future work.

*Surface-form sub-score calculators and production A/B.* The deterministic sub-score calculators use canonical-alias intersection and Jaccard overlap rather than embedding-based semantic similarity. A vacancy requiring Kubernetes and a CV listing Docker, Helm, and OpenShift score lower on the skill axis than the qualitative match would suggest. The Composite Judge is the design response — the model spots the equivalence — and what the Linear baseline misses is limited by surface-form matching. Separately, the v6 prompt is deployed to production; an A/B comparison against v4 under real-user click-through rates is recorded as the natural next step. The reason-quality evaluation is a strong predictor of user satisfaction in the cross-vendor judging literature, though it does not measure it directly.

*What would change the answer.* Three changes, in combination, would be sufficient to resolve the central thesis claim definitively. First, extending the CV gold set to at least thirty per role family would tighten the Layer 5 confidence interval enough to support per-family inference. Second, the Composite Judge calibration fix would remove the largest identifiable confound in the headline. Third, a multi-vendor judge ensemble would establish that the result is not an artefact of the single judge family chosen for the current evaluation. None of the three is conceptually difficult; all three are recorded as future work in @sec:conclusion.

*Held-out evaluation widens the picture but does not change the Layer 5 verdict.* Layer 6 adds an evaluation on 398 held-out pairs the production prompt never saw, with Spearman $rho = 0.65$ (95% CI $[0.58, 0.71]$) against a cross-vendor Claude Opus 4.7 gold, with $"NDCG"@5 = 0.82$ on top-of-list ranking, midrange-filter Spearman $rho = 0.738$ on the recruiter-workload regime, and a controlled ablation that empirically justifies the #raw("ScoringCapService") architectural decision. The held-out aggregate is consistent with the Layer 5 verdict and clarifies that the language-model architecture's advantage is conditional on the in-distribution recruiter workload.

== Chapter summary <sec:validation:summary>

In this chapter I described the layered evaluation methodology — the result of a mid-thesis pivot away from a structurally biased score-error endpoint — and the empirical findings it produced. Layers 1 and 2 are frozen at per-field scores of 0.896 (25 CVs) and 0.810 (355 of 357 vacancies). Layer 3 stays as a calibration diagnostic; the visible Linear-vs-Composite gap of 0.086 is explained by cap asymmetry, selection circularity, and a documented Composite Judge over-penalisation that has a one-line fix. Layer 4 shows a significant improvement of the v6 prompt over the v4 baseline on all four ordinal rubric dimensions (overall $Delta = +0.30$, 95 per cent CI $[+0.19, +0.40]$, $N approx 391$ pairs), judged by Claude Opus 4.7. Layer 5 — the primary endpoint of the earlier draft — is inconclusive on 14 CVs ($Delta$ NDCG\@10 $= -0.047$, CI $[-0.168, +0.065]$, judge Claude Sonnet 4.6); a post-hoc clamp shifts the headline to $+0.018$ without changing the verdict. Layer 6 — the held-out evaluation added late in the project — reports the production scoring system against a 398-pair held-out gold the prompt never saw, with Spearman $rho = 0.65$ (95% CI $[0.58, 0.71]$), $"NDCG"@5 = 0.82$, and midrange-filter Spearman $rho = 0.738$ on the recruiter-workload regime; the controlled #raw("ScoringCapService") ablation and the post-hoc isotonic calibrator (reducing Expected Calibration Error from $0.138$ to $0.027$ under 5-fold cross-validation, shipped to production) jointly justify the cap-and-calibrate architecture and retire the selection-circularity and vacancy-pool-leakage threats. The methodology stands regardless of the empirical verdict. The conditions under which the answer would tighten further are stated openly. Chapter 6 revisits the four objectives from the introduction and outlines the work that would close the remaining gaps.
